<!-- Theme — THE app's presentation vocabulary, once. Breakpoints, gutters, page shells,
     the brand palette, the locale-aware formatters and the shipped locale SET, as APP-WIDE
     FUNCTIONS every surface calls instead of re-deriving. It also carries the one piece of
     presentation that is state rather than vocabulary — restoring the viewer's saved UI
     language — because it is the component every screen already mounts first, and a cold
     link can land on any route.

     WHAT THIS REPLACES. Thirteen screens each carried a byte-identical copy of `bp`,
     `wide`, `gutter` and `shell` — AGENTS.md says "responsive is one vocabulary per
     screen", and it was one vocabulary fifteen times. Changing a breakpoint or the
     desktop gutter was a fifteen-file edit an adopter would get half of. Twenty distinct
     brand hexes were written out 529 times across 24 files, so a re-skin was a find-and-
     replace nobody could verify.

     THE MECHANISM is `<functions global="true">` — the framework's GLOBAL FUNCTION
     LIBRARY (OpenSource/Skills/js-core.md "Shared logic", corpus Conformance/functions).
     The block registers into the app-wide JSE table, last write wins, and a surface-local
     name shadows it. Wired on all three renderers, and — measured, which is the part that
     matters — on the SSR path too (`server/src/render.ts` renderInstance, same call as
     the dom layer`s mount.ts).

     ══ THE ONE RULE FOR A CALLER: MOUNT THIS FIRST ═══════════════════════════════════
     Registration happens when this component INSTANTIATES, not at app boot, so the mount
     has to come before the first read. Measured on a cold origin, first request:

       <Theme/> absent            /notices SSR rendered  BP[]        (a silent null)
       <Theme/> as first child    /notices SSR rendered  BP[phone]   (correct)

     A screen root reads its own computeds as soon as it evaluates a `visible-if` on its
     first child — `<TopNav visible-if="dsx.variable.wide"/>` is the usual one — so
     `<Theme/>` goes ABOVE that, as the literal first child of the screen root. It renders
     a 0×0 stack (the DemoSeed idiom) and paints nothing.

     ══ WHY THE PALETTE IS HALF-ROUTED, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A SHORTCUT ════════════════
     Every colour that a screen writes in MARKUP — an inline `color=`, a `background=`,
     a ternary in a computed, a hole in a `style=` string — calls a function here.

     Every colour inside a `<style as="…">` DECLARATION is still a literal, because a
     `<style>` attribute CANNOT carry an interpolation: the compiler drops the whole
     declaration, silently. Measured 2026-09-01 —

       <style as="a" color="{{ dsx.variable.brand }}" fontSize="15"/>
         → [data-dsx~="a562"] { font-size: 15px; }        (no `color` at all)

     — no error, no lint warning, no CSS. Filed as PLAN.md §6.91. Until it lands, the
     literals inside `<style>` blocks are CHECKED MIRRORS rather than sources: this file
     is the single definition and `npm run check:styles` refuses any hex in Components/**
     that is not one of the named tones below, so a mirror cannot drift silently and a
     re-skin cannot half-land. `npm run review` reads the same table, so the brand-palette
     waiver (§6.16) is derived from this file rather than from a second copy of it.

     Two of the tones below are unreachable from this file today and are named anyway:
     `railShellOf` is called with the 1440 CONTENT cap even on screens whose own shell is
     1100, because the rail is a full-bleed track and the page column is not — that is a
     real difference, not a copy that drifted. -->
<stack width="0" height="0" on:appear="dsx.action.restoreLocale()">
  <head>
    <!-- ══ THE PERSISTED CHOICE ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         `global.locale` is in-memory and dies with the page, so without this the picker
         is undone by every reload and every cold link. THE ONLY DECLARED CROSS-PLATFORM
         PLACE TO PUT IT IS THE COOKIE JAR — `dsx.cookie.*` is one grammar over
         document.cookie on web and DSXCookies on both native lanes — because there is
         still no key-value plane (PLAN.md §6.33, filed 2026-08-30, open).

         AND IT IS A SESSION COOKIE, which is a limitation and is named here rather than
         hidden: the web writer emits `name=value; path=/` with no max-age and no expires
         (dom/src/boot.ts wireCookies), so the choice survives reloads, deep links and tab
         restores and does NOT survive quitting the browser. Measured, not assumed. The
         Language row says so in the UI. Filed as PLAN.md §6.100.

         Restoring here rather than in one screen is deliberate: a viewer can cold-land on
         any route, so the restore belongs to the component every screen already mounts
         first. It is idempotent and it never invents a choice — an absent cookie leaves
         `global.locale` alone, which leaves the kernel ladder on its device step. -->
    <action as="restoreLocale">
      const saved = dsx.cookie.uiLocale
      if (saved == null) { return }
      if (saved == '') { return }
      if (global.locale == saved) { return }
      // A COOKIE IS UNTRUSTED INPUT even when this app wrote it: a tag from an older build,
      // a hand-edited jar or a shared domain would otherwise pin the app to a language it
      // cannot render, and the failure is invisible (every lookup misses, so it reads as
      // plain English with a picker insisting otherwise).
      if (localeTags().indexOf(saved) == -1) { return }
      global.locale = saved
    </action>
    <!-- BODY CAVEAT (js-core.md): the NATIVE parsers do not lift a `<functions>` body as
         raw JS yet, so a bare `<` in here breaks them and lint mirrors that by law. Every
         comparison below is therefore spelled with `>=` or `!=`, never `<`, and there is
         no `&&` — an XML body would need it escaped and the escape is easy to lose. -->
    <functions global="true">
      /* ── THE RESPONSIVE VOCABULARY ────────────────────────────────────────────────
         phone 0-767 · tablet 768-1119 · desktop 1120+. The same three names the router
         uses for its motion breakpoint (dsx.config.json router.motionBreakpoint 768) and
         the same the native export puts iPad portrait and iPad Pro landscape on. */
      function bpOf(w) {
        if (w >= 1120) { return 'desktop' }
        if (w >= 768) { return 'tablet' }
        return 'phone'
      }

      /* every screen asked the same question of bp, so it is one name now */
      function isWide(bp) { return bp != 'phone' }

      /* the two-column player collapses at 1120 — ShortMax collapses at 1024 and
         ReelShort keeps a 416 panel; 1120 is this app's measured choice (AGENTS.md) */
      function panelAt(w) { return w >= 1120 }

      /* MEASURED, not chosen: 86 is the category reference gutter on desktop. */
      function gutterOf(bp) {
        if (bp == 'desktop') { return 86 }
        if (bp == 'tablet') { return 40 }
        return 16
      }

      /* the OPERATOR gutter is deliberately tighter — Manage is a dense table surface,
         not a storefront, and 86px of air on a data grid is wasted column */
      function opsGutterOf(bp) {
        if (bp == 'desktop') { return 32 }
        if (bp == 'tablet') { return 24 }
        return 16
      }

      /* THE PAGE SHELL: a centred column capped at maxW with the gutter as its padding.
         maxW is the caller`s, because it is a CONTENT decision — 1440 for a storefront
         grid, 1100 for a purchase column, 760 for an inbox, 520 for a credential form. */
      function shellOf(bp, maxW) {
        const g = gutterOf(bp)
        return 'width: 100%; max-width: ' + maxW + 'px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; padding-left: ' + g + 'px; padding-right: ' + g + 'px'
      }

      function opsShellOf(bp, maxW) {
        const g = opsGutterOf(bp)
        return 'width: 100%; max-width: ' + maxW + 'px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; padding-left: ' + g + 'px; padding-right: ' + g + 'px'
      }

      /* A HORIZONTAL RAIL`S TRACK IS `max-content`, never the page shell`s `width: 100%`
         (AGENTS.md): the shell recipe caps the track at the scroller and the rows eat the
         difference — measured, 150px posters rendered at 72.25px. The lead padding keeps
         the first card flush with the page column on a viewport wider than the cap. */
      function railShellOf(bp, w, maxW) {
        const g = gutterOf(bp)
        const over = w - maxW
        const lead = g + (over > 0 ? Math.round(over / 2) : 0)
        return 'width: max-content; padding-left: ' + lead + 'px; padding-right: ' + lead + 'px'
      }

      /* ── THE BRAND PALETTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Named by JOB, not by hue, so a re-skin is a value change and never a rename.
         Callers: markup only (see the header — a `<style>` cannot hold an interpolation).
         `npm run check:styles` proves no hex in Components/** is outside this table. */

      /* the accent. One job: the thing you tap, the thing that is on. */
      function brand() { return '#FF2C55' }
      function brandTint() { return '#FF6B8A' }      /* accent on a dark card, links */
      function brandPressed() { return '#8E1637' }   /* the followed/pressed state */
      function brandSoft() { return '#FF8FA8' }      /* accent ink on a coloured wash */

      /* money. gold is VIP, coin is a balance — two different promises, two tones. */
      function gold() { return '#F6B63D' }
      function coin() { return '#F7C948' }
      function goldHi() { return '#FFE9A8' }
      function vipGoldTop() { return '#F7E3AE' }
      function vipGoldMid() { return '#E8C77A' }
      function vipGoldEnd() { return '#DDB35E' }
      function onGold() { return '#3A2A08' }         /* ink on a gold card */
      function onGoldSoft() { return '#2A1D04' }

      /* the stage and the ink */
      function stage() { return '#000000' }
      function ink() { return '#FFFFFF' }
      function inkOnLight() { return '#111111' }

      /* the dark surface ramp, deepest first */
      function surfaceDeep() { return '#0A0A0E' }
      function surface() { return '#141419' }
      function surfaceRaised() { return '#17171C' }
      function surfaceCard() { return '#1C1C22' }
      function surfaceInput() { return '#1E1E24' }
      function surfaceEdge() { return '#1F1F26' }
      function surfaceChip() { return '#232329' }
      function surfaceChipOn() { return '#26262C' }
      function surfaceHair() { return '#3A3A42' }
      function surfaceHairLift() { return '#43434D' }

      /* the sheet ramp (the DramaWave drawer set — a sheet sits ABOVE the page, so it
         gets its own neutrals rather than borrowing the page`s) */
      function sheet() { return '#141414' }
      function sheetRaised() { return '#1A1A1C' }
      function sheetCard() { return '#1C1C1E' }
      function sheetChip() { return '#242426' }
      function sheetEdge() { return '#2A2A2E' }
      function sheetCell() { return '#2C2C2E' }

      /* ── THE REFERENCE PALETTE (docs/design/reference-ui-spec.md §0) ─────────────────
         The founder's reference app, transcribed: the redesigned Home, Explore, My List and
         the five-tab bar paint these and only these. Named by job, like everything above;
         `sheet()` / `sheetCard()` / `sheetCell()` already ARE the spec's panel / card / chip
         tones (#141414 / #1C1C1E / #2C2C2E), so those three are not repeated here. */
      function accent() { return '#FF2C55' }         /* = brand(): the ReelShort pink/red — the founder's call (2026-09-02) over the reference app's teal — for the current episode, the timer ring, a required mark, the checkbox, the coin callout */
      function accentWidget() { return '#FF3D5D' }   /* the Live Activity / widget CTA: ReelShort's own player pink (measured, AGENTS.md), a step lighter on the #0E0E10 card; white ink on it */
      function crownGold() { return '#F5C518' }      /* the crown, plan titles, % OFF badges, unlock-pill text */
      function ctaGold() { return '#F5D33D' }        /* the membership CTA fill */
      function goldDim() { return '#3B3410' }        /* the unlock pill fill */
      function hotRed() { return '#E0342C' }         /* the HOT and NEW badges */
      function planSelected() { return '#FF2C55' }   /* the selected plan card outline — the accent, not the reference app's green */
      function savedMark() { return '#FF2C55' }      /* the player's saved bookmark — the accent, not the reference app's lime */
      function ink2() { return '#C7C7CC' }           /* synopsis body, locked episode numbers */
      function ink3() { return '#8E8E93' }           /* secondary labels, inactive tabs, footnotes */
      function grabber() { return '#48484A' }        /* the sheet grabber, a checkbox outline at rest */
      function toggleOff() { return '#39393D' }      /* a toggle track at rest */
      function widgetCard() { return '#0E0E10' }     /* the Live Activity card */

      /* ── THE REWARDS PAGE (docs/design/reference-ui-spec.md §9) ────────────────────
         Read off the founder reference set at 1206×2622 and named by JOB. The page has
         its own neutral ramp — a #0E0E0E ground under #1A1A1A cards, #161616 row cards
         and #262626 cells — because the reference draws it one step lighter than the
         storefront black, and the two lanes must stay pixel-identical (spec −1). */
      function rewardsGround() { return '#0E0E0E' }   /* the page ground under the two tabs */
      function rewardsCard() { return '#1A1A1A' }     /* the check-in card, the membership card, the redeem cards, the modals, the rules sheet */
      function rewardsCell() { return '#262626' }     /* a check-in day cell */
      function rewardsRow() { return '#161616' }      /* an Earn Rewards row card */
      function rewardsTile() { return '#2A2A2A' }     /* the 44pt icon tile, the unaffordable Redeem pill, the phone-mock plate */
      function warmGold() { return '#F6D27A' }        /* the glyph inside a row tile */
      function timelineTrack() { return '#3A3A3A' }   /* the membership timeline track */
      function plum() { return '#6A2A7A' }            /* the Member Points hero wash, second stop */
      function plumDeep() { return '#7A2A50' }        /* the redeem tile gradient end */
      function ember() { return '#FF6A3D' }           /* the success CTA gradient end */
      function wine() { return '#3A1030' }            /* the Coming Soon band, first stop */

      /* WHERE THE CROWN GOES. The crown button on Home, the unlock pills and the plans all
         open the Membership page (spec 6): /membership, the route Components/Membership.dsx
         serves — the store catalogue's three plans, Core/Store through RevenueCat on a device
         and Stripe on the web (Components/parts/BuyButton.dsx). Every crown reads the
         destination from here so a rename is ONE edit rather than a hunt. */
      function membershipPath() { return '/membership' }

      /* one-off brand moments, each earned */
      function recoveryViolet() { return '#1E1830' }  /* the Stripe settle-recovery card */
      function violetEdge() { return '#3A3547' }
      function positive() { return '#5FD08A' }        /* a grant landed */
      function rankSilver() { return '#B9C5D8' }
      function rankBronze() { return '#CE8B5C' }
      function gemLift() { return '#FF7BAA' }         /* the VIP gem gradient, top stop */
      function gemDeep() { return '#B3103A' }         /* ... and its end stop */
      function bargainEnd() { return '#FF6A3D' }      /* the "Good Bargain" corner badge: accent → this (spec §10) */


      /* ── THE VIP PROMISE, SAID ONCE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
         Product copy in the presentation library needs a reason, and this is it: a PAID
         PROMISE is the one string an app must never hold two copies of. The benefit list
         and the term sentence are rendered on two surfaces that a viewer can reach within
         one tap of each other — the /vip page and the plans sheet that a nested level now
         opens over — and an adopter who edits one is an adopter who has shipped an app
         that advertises two different products at two different prices.

         Every row below is a thing `server/*.dsx` ACTUALLY GRANTS, and can be checked:
           unlocked  `unlockEpisode` prices at 0 while `vip_until` is in the future
                     (server/wallet.dsx) — no catalogue exceptions, so it covers shows
                     added after the pass was bought
           coins     because that price is 0 the spend path never runs: no ledger row, no
                     wallet debit, so the balance survives the pass
           no ads    <AdGate> takes `vip=` and refuses in its own action
         Three rows and not four. "1080p" and "Offline downloads" were deleted in an
         earlier pass because nothing in this app delivers either; padding the list back
         to four would re-create the defect that removal fixed. */
      /* FOUR ROWS NOW, and the fourth is still a server fact rather than a fill: the
         membership cards wear the category`s 2×2 benefits grid (spec §10), whose reference
         cells read "Unlimited viewing · Ad-Free · 1080P quality · Download" — and the last
         two are exactly the rows this list deleted, for the reasons above. So the fourth cell
         is the one claim `unlockEpisode` makes that b1 only implied: the pass has NO
         catalogue exceptions, so a series added tomorrow is unlocked the moment it lands.
         `short` is the grid cell`s word (15pt beside an 18pt glyph in a 160pt cell); `label`
         and `note` are the row form the /vip page, the Membership rewards list and the plans
         sheet`s detail level print. */
      /* THROUGH localize(): a data row in a <functions> block is not a display point, so
         these words never reached the tables and rendered English inside every locale
         (found while adding the grid). The kernel`s own JSE verb resolves them here, the
         extractor reads the literal as a key (scripts/strings.mjs localizeCallKeys), and
         the languageRows() device row two functions down is the precedent. */
      function vipBenefits() {
        return [
          { id: 'b1', icon: 'lock.open.fill',         short: localize('Unlimited viewing'),   label: localize('Every episode unlocked'),
            note: localize('Paid episodes open at 0 coins, on every series in the catalogue') },
          { id: 'b2', icon: 'bolt.fill',              short: localize('Ad-free'),             label: localize('No ads'),
            note: localize('The one rewarded ad this app shows is switched off while your pass is active') },
          { id: 'b3', icon: 'dollarsign.circle.fill', short: localize('Coins untouched'),     label: localize('Your coins stay yours'),
            note: localize('A VIP unlock debits nothing, so the balance you bought is still there when the pass ends') },
          { id: 'b4', icon: 'sparkles',               short: localize('New series included'), label: localize('New series included'),
            note: localize('A pass has no catalogue exceptions, so a series added while it runs is unlocked the moment it lands') }
        ]
      }

      /* ── THE LIMITED OFFER CLOCK (spec §10) ─────────────────────────────────────────
         The price list carries `offerEndsAt` on a tier while an operator`s offer row is in
         the future (server/store.dsx); the card wears a countdown badge until then. Both
         halves read the SAME two facts — the ISO end and a ticking `now` the screen keeps —
         so a card cannot show a clock the server did not send. The digits are grown from a
         non-numeric anchor because JSE `+` is total arithmetic: `'0' + 5` is the number 5,
         `'x0' + 5` is the string x05, and the anchor is sliced off (AGENTS.md). No `<` and
         no `&&` in this block (the body caveat above). */
      function offerLive(endsAt, now) {
        if (endsAt == null) { return false }
        if (endsAt == '') { return false }
        return new Date(endsAt).getTime() - now >= 1000
      }
      function twoDigits(n) {
        let out = 'x'
        if (n >= 10) { out = out + n } else { out = out + '0'; out = out + n }
        return out.slice(1)
      }
      function countdownText(endsAt, now) {
        if (offerLive(endsAt, now) != true) { return '' }
        const left = Math.floor((new Date(endsAt).getTime() - now) / 1000)
        const h = Math.floor(left / 3600)
        const m = Math.floor((left - h * 3600) / 60)
        const s = left - h * 3600 - m * 60
        let out = twoDigits(h) + ':'
        out = out + twoDigits(m)
        out = out + ':'
        out = out + twoDigits(s)
        return out
      }

      /* ── THE CLAIMABLE TOTAL, ONE ARITHMETIC ────────────────────────────────────────
         The coins a viewer can claim RIGHT NOW with a tap, from the SERVER facts in
         /rewards/state: the day`s check-in reward while it is unclaimed, every completed
         task not yet claimed, every proven invitee not yet swept, and the rewarded ads
         still available (a non-member`s lane). The once-ever rows (sign-in, push) land
         their grant without a tap, so they are never "to claim" — `claimed` flips the
         moment the ledger row exists.
         The Rewards tab prints it and the tab bar`s callout binds the same function over
         the app-wide stash (global.cacheRewards), so the two never disagree. A null state
         is 0, never a guess. Statements rather than `+`-joined expressions, and no `&&`,
         for the body caveat above. */
      function rewardsClaimable(state, vip) {
        if (state == null) { return 0 }
        let total = 0
        if (state.checkedToday == false) { total = total + Number(state.nextReward) }
        if (state.tasks != null) {
          for (const t of state.tasks) {
            if (t.claimed != true) {
              if (Number(t.progress) >= Number(t.target)) { total = total + Number(t.reward) }
              /* the invite row: every proven invitee not yet swept is a tap away */
              if (t.pending != null) { total = total + Number(t.pending) * Number(t.reward) }
            }
          }
        }
        if (vip != true) {
          if (state.adsLeft != null) { total = total + Number(state.adsLeft) * Number(state.adReward) }
        }
        return total
      }

      /* THE TERM, derived from the tiers the SERVER shipped rather than authored — so the
         day a deployment changes its pass lengths this sentence changes with them. Pass
         `catalog.vip` (or null while it is still loading). */
      function vipTermLine(tiers) {
        const tail = ' When the date passes VIP simply stops — your coins are not affected.'
        if (tiers == null) { return 'Every plan is a one-time charge for a pass that runs a fixed number of days.' + tail }
        if (tiers.length == 0) { return 'Every plan is a one-time charge for a pass that runs a fixed number of days.' + tail }
        let days = ''
        for (const t of tiers) { days = days == '' ? String(t.days) : days + ', ' + String(t.days) }
        return 'Every plan is a one-time charge for a dated pass — ' + days + ' days. Nothing renews and there is nothing to cancel; buying again adds days to the pass you hold.' + tail
      }

      /* ── LOCALE-AWARE FORMATTING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Numbers and dates are locale FORMATTING, not translation, and the string table
         cannot help with either: `1.2M` and `2026-09-01` are composed at render time and
         resolve before the lookup. JSE carries the real thing — Intl.NumberFormat,
         Intl.DateTimeFormat and toLocaleDateString are all in the builtin table on every
         renderer — so these are the twins of the four hand-rolled formatters this template
         used to carry (`Math.round(v / 100) / 10 + 'M'` and `String(iso).slice(0, 10)`,
         which print an American number and an ISO date to every reader on earth). */

      /* the ACTIVE locale, or empty for "let the device decide" — the same ladder the
         kernel string seam walks (global.locale, then the device, then en) */
      function activeLocale() {
        const l = global.locale
        if (l == null) { return '' }
        return String(l)
      }

      /* ── THE DIRECTION VOCABULARY, AND WHY THE APP HAS TO OWN ONE ─────────────────
         The framework RESOLVES the layout direction (DSXStrings.direction(): an app pin
         over the locale, script subtag over language) and applies it at each root — web
         `dir=`, SwiftUI `.environment(\layoutDirection)`, Compose LocalLayoutDirection.
         Everything that is a LAYOUT fact then mirrors for free, and measured, it does:
         the tab bar reverses, `align="leading"` moves to the right edge, rails start at
         the right, logical padding swaps.

         AN ICON NAME IS NOT A LAYOUT FACT. `chevron.left` is a shape, the shared catalog
         (Conformance/icons/sf-map.json, 115 names) has no logical pair to swap it for —
         no `chevron.forward`/`chevron.backward` — and no renderer mirrors a physical
         name. Measured in Arabic at 390px: all 37 directional icons in this template
         pointed the wrong way, back chevrons included. So the app picks the GLYPH, which
         is the one mirroring mechanism that works identically on all three lanes.

         AND THE RESOLVED DIRECTION IS NOT READABLE FROM DSX. The plane publishes nothing
         into the store, and `global.locale` is empty EXACTLY when the device is deciding
         — so this function knows the direction of a choice made IN THE APP and cannot
         know the direction of an Arabic device that never opened the picker. That device
         gets a correctly mirrored layout from the engine and unmirrored chevrons from
         this app. Named in the README, filed as PLAN.md §6.102, and deliberately not
         papered over with a boot-time write of the device tag into `global.locale` —
         that would delete the kernel ladder's device step for every locale at once. */
      /* THE READING-DIRECTION GLYPHS ARE THE CATALOG'S JOB NOW, and five functions went
         with them. This library used to carry `rtlLangs`, `isRtl`, `chevBack`, `chevFwd`
         and `arrowFwd`, and 34 call sites interpolated them — 34 chances to forget, in one
         app, with every app after it owing the same tax. The shared icon catalog gained a
         logical vocabulary (`chevron.backward` / `chevron.forward` / `arrow.forward`, SF
         Symbols' own spelling), each name carrying the physical twin whose glyph it copies
         field for field, and each renderer turns it against the layout direction it already
         holds. So the call sites are literals again and the direction is resolved where the
         direction is known.
         This also retires PLAN.md §6.102 FOR ICONS: `isRtl` read `global.locale`, which is
         empty exactly when the DEVICE is deciding, so an Arabic device that never opened the
         picker got a mirrored layout from the engine and unmirrored chevrons from this app.
         Nothing in the app reads the direction now, so nothing can read it wrongly. §6.102
         stays open for the transform and gesture cases, which still have no DSX-readable
         direction. */

      /* a view count at glance width. The K/M suffixes stay Latin because the compact
         notation Intl would give us is NOT in the framework number formatter (its options
         are style/currency/fraction digits/grouping), so inventing `notation: compact`
         here would work on web and silently print the raw number on a device. What IS
         portable is the NUMERAL, which is what actually differs: es-ES writes 1,2 M. */
      function compactCount(v) {
        if (v == null) { return '' }
        if (v >= 1000000) { return decimalOne(v / 1000000) + 'M' }
        if (v >= 1000) { return decimalOne(v / 1000) + 'K' }
        return String(v)
      }

      function decimalOne(n) {
        const loc = activeLocale()
        const opts = { maximumFractionDigits: 1 }
        if (loc == '') { return new Intl.NumberFormat(undefined, opts).format(n) }
        return new Intl.NumberFormat(loc, opts).format(n)
      }

      /* an ISO timestamp as a date the reader recognises. Slicing the first ten characters
         gave every reader 2026-09-01; this gives 01/09/2026 in es and 9/1/2026 in en-US. */
      function shortDate(iso) {
        if (iso == null) { return '' }
        if (iso == '') { return '' }
        const loc = activeLocale()
        if (loc == '') { return new Date(iso).toLocaleDateString() }
        return new Date(iso).toLocaleDateString(loc)
      }

      /* ── THE SHIPPED LOCALE SET ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
         ONE list, because two would drift: the language picker renders it, and
         `npm run verify` reads the SAME rows out of this file and asserts that every
         tag names a table the build actually folded into the registry. So a row here
         with no `Strings.<tag>.json` beside it fails the build rather than offering a
         viewer a language that silently answers English.

         EVERY LABEL IS AN ENDONYM and none of them is ever translated. That is not a
         shortcut around the string seam — it is the correct answer: a Japanese reader
         hunting for German is looking for `Deutsch`, not for `ドイツ語`. It also happens
         to be the only answer available, because a choice control`s option labels never
         reach the localization seam at all (PLAN.md §6.98).

         THE TAG IS THE FILENAME, lowercase, and the kernel ladder tries the full tag
         then the bare language — so `de` also catches de-AT and de-CH, while `pt-br`
         and `zh-hant` are reachable from this picker and NOT from a device whose system
         language is pt-PT or zh-TW (also §6.98).

         `ar` IS THE ROW THAT TURNS THE APP AROUND, and it needs no code here. Picking it
         writes `global.locale = 'ar'` exactly like every other row; the framework's
         direction plane reads that SAME state through DSXStrings.direction() and sets
         `dir="rtl"` on the document element (web), `.environment(\layoutDirection)` (iOS)
         and `LocalLayoutDirection` (Compose). Text and layout cannot disagree because
         they resolve one locale. What that plane does NOT mirror is named in the README
         and in PLAN.md §6.101-§6.103. */
      function localeTags() {
        return ['en', 'es', 'pt-br', 'ja', 'de', 'fr', 'ko', 'zh-hant', 'id', 'th', 'it', 'tr', 'vi', 'ar']
      }

      function languageRows() {
        return [
          { id: '', label: localize('Device language') },
          { id: 'en', label: 'English' },
          { id: 'es', label: 'Español' },
          { id: 'pt-br', label: 'Português (Brasil)' },
          { id: 'ja', label: '日本語' },
          { id: 'de', label: 'Deutsch' },
          { id: 'fr', label: 'Français' },
          { id: 'ko', label: '한국어' },
          { id: 'zh-hant', label: '繁體中文' },
          { id: 'id', label: 'Bahasa Indonesia' },
          { id: 'th', label: 'ไทย' },
          { id: 'it', label: 'Italiano' },
          { id: 'tr', label: 'Türkçe' },
          { id: 'vi', label: 'Tiếng Việt' },
          { id: 'ar', label: 'العربية' }
        ]
      }

      /* THE ONE ROW THAT IS A SENTENCE RATHER THAN A NAME, and therefore the one row the
         endonym argument above does not cover. It reaches the table through localize() —
         the kernel's own JSE verb since dev@d2927f31 (§6.98 resolved) — so the thirteen
         hand-kept copies this function used to carry are one key in Strings.<tag>.json,
         harvested by scripts/strings.mjs like any display attribute. The active locale is
         empty exactly when the device is deciding, and a miss renders the English key, the
         same fall-through as every other miss in this app (Article 7). */
    </functions>
  </head>
</stack>
